    "explanation": "Q1 computes the minimum absolute difference between all distinct pairs of points (A.X ≠ B.X). Q2 computes the minimum absolute difference between each point and its next one or next two points in sorted order via LEAD with offsets 1 and 2. The divergence occurs because Q2 only considers differences between points that are adjacent or separated by one point in the sorted order, while Q1 considers all possible distinct pairs. For example, if the minimum difference exists between points that are not adjacent or separated by one position in sorted order (e.g., between the first and third points when sorted), Q2 will miss it, leading to different results.",
    "counterexample_dsp": "Test data with three points where the minimum absolute difference is between the first and third points (not adjacent or separated by one in sorted order). For points [1, 3, 4], Q1 considers all pairs: |1-3|=2, |1-4|=3, |3-4|=1 → MIN=1. Q2 only considers: LEAD offset 1: |1-3|=2, |3-4|=1, LEAD offset 2: |1-4|=3 → MIN=1 (same here, but note Q2 misses some pairs). To show difference, use points [1, 4, 5]: Q1 pairs: |1-4|=3, |1-5|=4, |4-5|=1 → MIN=1. Q2: LEAD offset 1: |1-4|=3, |4-5|=1; LEAD offset 2: |1-5|=4 → MIN=1 (still same). Actually, the difference manifests when the minimum is between non-adjacent points in sorted order, and that minimum is smaller than any adjacent or one-apart differences. For points [1, 10, 11]: Q1: |1-10|=9, |1-11|=10, |10-11|=1 → MIN=1. Q2: LEAD offset 1: |1-10|=9, |10-11|=1; LEAD offset 2: |1-11|=10 → MIN=1 (same). To force difference, need a case where the minimum difference is between points that are not covered by LEAD offsets 1 or 2. For example, points [1, 100, 101, 102] with min diff between 101 and 102 (adjacent, so Q2 catches it). The issue is Q2's LEAD offsets only look ahead 1 or 2 positions; if the min diff is between points further apart (e.g., positions 1 and 4 in sorted order), Q2 misses it. For points [1, 5, 6, 100]: sorted [1,5,6,100]. Q1 considers all pairs, min diff is |5-6|=1. Q2: LEAD offset 1: |1-5|=4, |5-6|=1, |6-100|=94; LEAD offset 2: |1-6|=5, |5-100|=95 → MIN=1 (same). To show difference, the min diff must be between points not adjacent or one apart. For points [1, 2, 100, 101]: sorted [1,2,100,101]. Q1: pairs include |1-2|=1, |100-101|=1, etc. → MIN=1. Q2: LEAD offset 1: |1-2|=1, |2-100|=98, |100-101|=1; LEAD offset 2: |1-100|=99, |2-101|=99 → MIN=1 (same). The key is that Q2's UNION ALL combines two sets: differences with next point and with next-next point. It misses differences between points that are further apart (e.g., positions 1 and 3 if not covered by offset 2? Actually offset 2 covers position 1 to 3). Wait, for sorted points, LEAD(X,2) gives the value two rows ahead. So for points [a,b,c,d], it computes |a-c| and |b-d|, missing |a-d|. Thus, if the min diff is between first and last points (positions 1 and 4), Q2 misses it. Example: points [1, 50, 51, 52]. Sorted: [1,50,51,52]. Q1: all pairs, min diff is |50-51|=1 or |51-52|=1. Q2 catches |50-51|=1 via LEAD offset 1. To show difference, need min diff between points that are not within 2 positions in sorted order. For points [1, 10, 20, 21]: sorted [1,10,20,21]. Q1: min diff is |20-21|=1. Q2: LEAD offset 1: |1-10|=9, |10-20|=10, |20-21|=1; LEAD offset 2: |1-20|=19, |10-21|=11 → MIN=1 (same). The only way Q2 misses a pair is if the points are more than 2 positions apart. For min diff to be between such a pair, the closer pairs must have larger differences. Example: points [1, 100, 200, 201]. Sorted [1,100,200,201]. Q1: pairs include |200-201|=1 (min). Q2 catches |200-201|=1 via LEAD offset 1. To make Q2 miss the min, the min must be between, say, positions 1 and 4: |1-201|=200, not min. So design points where the min diff is between positions 1 and 4, and all closer pairs have larger diffs. For points [1, 1000, 1001, 1002]: sorted [1,1000,1001,1002]. Q1: min diff is |1000-1001|=1. Q2 catches it. Need points where the min diff is exactly between positions 1 and 4: e.g., [1, 100, 200, 2]. Sorted: [1,2,100,200]. Q1: min diff is |1-2|=1. Q2 catches it. To achieve: let min diff be between first and last, and make all other diffs larger. For points [1, 100, 101, 2]: sorted [1,2,100,101]. Q1: min diff is |1-2|=1. Q2 catches it. The only way is if the min diff is between points that are exactly 3 positions apart in sorted order (since LEAD offset 2 covers up to 2 apart). For 4 points sorted [a,b,c,d], differences computed by Q2: |a-b|, |b-c|, |c-d|, |a-c|, |b-d|. Missing |a-d|. So if |a-d| is the minimum, Q2 will output a larger value. Example: points [1, 5, 6, 2]. Sorted: [1,2,5,6]. Q1: all pairs,