s 1, 100, 200, 2, 300, sorted is [1,2,100,200,300] so 2 is position 2. To have 2 at position 4, we need three values less than 2, but 1 is the only one less than 2. So impossible with integers since 1 and 2 are consecutive integers and will be adjacent in sorted order. Therefore, to have the min diff between non-adjacent points, the values must not be consecutive integers. Example: points [1, 4, 7, 2]. Sorted: [1,2,4,7]. Q1: pairs include |1-2|=1, |2-4|=2, |4-7|=3, |1-4|=3, |1-7|=6, |2-7|=5 → MIN=1. Q2: LEAD offset 1: |1-2|=1, |2-4|=2, |4-7|=3; LEAD offset 2: |1-4|=3, |2-7|=5 → MIN=1. Still same. The difference occurs when the min diff is not between points that are within 2 positions in sorted order. For that, the min diff must be larger than the differences within 2 positions, but then it's not the min. Contradiction. Wait, Q2 might miss a pair that has the minimum difference if that pair is not covered by its LEAD offsets. But if that pair has the minimum difference, then by definition, all other pairs have differences >= that min. The pairs covered by Q2 are a subset of all pairs. So if the min diff pair is not in Q2's subset, then Q2 will output the min of the subset, which is > the true min. So we need a set where the true min diff pair is not in Q2's subset. For example, with 4 points sorted [a,b,c,d], Q2 covers pairs (a,b), (b,c), (c,d), (a,c), (b,d). The missing pair is (a,d). So if |a-d| is the smallest among all pairs, then Q2 will output min(|a-b|, |b-c|, |c-d|, |a-c|, |b-d|) which is > |a-d|. So we need |a-d| < all other differences. Example: a=1, d=3, then |a-d|=2. Choose b and c such that |a-b|>2, |b-c|>2, etc. Let b=10, c=20. Then sorted [1,10,20,3] actually sorts to [1,3,10,20]. Then a=1, d=20? No, in sorted order, positions are: 1:1, 2:3, 3:10, 4:20. We want positions 1 and 4 to be closest: |1-20|=19, which is not less than |1-3|=2. To have |1-20| be smallest, we need |1-3|, |3-10|, etc. all >19. Impossible since |1-3|=2. So to have |a-d| as min, a